      "summary": "Short-term solar power forecasting with a hybrid CNN–Transformer model.",
      "description": "Solar energy production forecasting is critical for the safe, stable, and economical operation \nof modern renewable energy grids. However, the high dependence of solar energy on weather \nconditions, particularly dynamic cloud cover, relative humidity, and temperature, leads to \nsudden, non-linear fluctuations in generation. While traditional physical and statistical models \nfail to capture these complex interactions, standalone deep learning models (such as CNN, \nLSTM, or Transformer alone) suffer from limitations like memory loss, sequential processing \nconstraints, or overfitting to local noise. In this thesis, an optimized Hybrid CNN-Transformer \ndeep learning model was developed, combining spatial feature extraction with a long-term \ntemporal attention mechanism. The study utilized hourly meteorological and generation data \nobtained from the Kaggle platform. During the data preprocessing stage, a true cyclical time-of-day encoding, MinMax normalization fitted solely to the training set, and a 48-\nhour sliding window transformation were applied within a chronological train-test split to \nprevent data leakage between overlapping windows. In the proposed model, local weather \npatterns were extracted at full resolution (without temporal downsampling) using a Conv1D \n+ Batch Normalization block; sinusoidal positional encoding was added to preserve the \ntemporal sequence; and daily peak values and seasonal cycles were subsequently learned via \na compact 4-head Multi-Head Attention mechanism.",
